Good — that constraint actually leads to a simpler design, not a worse one. Instead of generating a random string and needing somewhere durable to store it, derive keyLocation deterministically from job.getId(), which is already persisted, already unique, and already available everywhere via ChunkMessage.getJobId(). No entity change needed at all.

Why this works as well as (or better than) a stored random value
job.getId() is already a unique, durable, auto-generated identifier — every requirement a new panKeyLocation field would have given us, it already satisfies.
Every stage — ChunkGeneratorController, SaveTriggeredConsumerDataImpl, FullfillerDefault, ShipfileBuilderImpl — can independently recompute the same value from job.getId() / cm.getJobId(), with zero propagation needed. That's actually more robust than passing a generated value through ChunkMessage.encPanCsvPath, since there's no window where one stage could see a stale or missing value.
"Always generate new" is satisfied automatically: a new job has a new id, so it always gets a fresh keyLocation — no explicit generation step required at dispatch time at all.
One trade-off worth naming honestly: job.getId() is a sequential integer, so "job-" + id is a predictable Vault path — anyone who can reach Vault (past auth) could enumerate job IDs and guess valid paths ahead of time. That's a smaller risk than it sounds (Vault's real security boundary is the AppRole token, not path secrecy), but if you want non-guessability too, salt it with a fixed per-environment constant so the path isn't purely sequential:

