private void generateEncPanCsv(String custCode, String outFileName, Job job) {
    String keyLocation = PanSubstitutionService.deriveKeyLocationForJob(job.getId());

    Map<String, String> plainPanMap = resolvePlainPanMapForOutput(keyLocation, outFileName);
    if (!plainPanMap.isEmpty()) {
        Map<String, String> encryptedMap = buildEncryptedMap(keyLocation, keyLocation, plainPanMap);
        if (!encryptedMap.isEmpty()) {
            writeEncPanCsv(outFileName, keyLocation, keyLocation, encryptedMap);
            finalizeJob(custCode, keyLocation, null);
            return;
        }
        log.warn("encrypted map empty for [{}], attempting intermediate CSV recovery", keyLocation);
    } else {
        log.warn("warm cache empty for [{}], attempting intermediate CSV recovery", keyLocation);
    }

    recoverFromIntermediateCsvs(custCode, outFileName);
}
Note the keyLocation == null branch from the previous version is gone — deriveKeyLocationForJob can never return null since it's a pure string derivation, not a lookup. This actually removes a failure mode: there's no more "job's keyLocation field wasn't set" case to handle, since nothing needs setting in the first place.

resolvePlainPanMapForOutput, buildEncryptedMap, writeEncPanCsv, finalizeJob, recoverFromIntermediateCsvs — all unchanged from the previous version.

Net effect of this constraint
Removing the Job.panKeyLocation field requirement actually simplified the design: no schema migration, no risk of the field being null/unset for in-flight jobs created before the change, and no dependency on ChunkMessage.encPanCsvPath being correctly propagated — every single call site derives the same value independently and correctly, permanently, from data that already exists.

